        <div class="section">
            <div class="section-intro">
                <div class="section-eyebrow section-eyebrow-future">Looking forward</div>
                <h2>How long does the bitcoin advantage last?</h2>
                <p><em>The forward projection &mdash; what the Power Law trend says about the next 10 to 30 years.</em></p>
            </div>

            <p class="prose">The retrospective evidence above answers the question for someone considering a position who would have started at any of the dates the page covers. The forward question for someone deciding right now is: <strong>over the horizon I&rsquo;ll actually hold this, where is the trend headed?</strong></p>

            <p class="prose">The chart below plots <strong>two</strong> bitcoin paths against the long-run trend lines for the S&amp;P 500 and the NASDAQ-100: the same two scenarios for bitcoin&rsquo;s price that every forward calculator on the site uses. In the first (solid), bitcoin stays as far from its Power Law trend as it is today and grows at the trend&rsquo;s own rate: the conservative read when bitcoin is below trend, since nothing in it depends on a recovery, and the optimistic one when it is above. In the second (dashed), the gap closes in a straight line over 3 years and bitcoin then follows the trend: the historical pattern, since every earlier stretch on either side of the trend has ended with bitcoin back at it. Both start at the amount invested today, so <strong>the distance that opens between them over the first 3 years is what today&rsquo;s position against the trend amounts to if that pattern holds</strong>: the dashed line pulls ahead when bitcoin starts below trend and falls behind when it starts above. Neither is a forecast. Bitcoin has spent long stretches on either side of the trend, and deviations within cycles can be very large in either direction. The stock lines run at their long-run averages; the stress test above the chart switches them to their own record since 2010, the years bitcoin has had a market price, which makes the stock market&rsquo;s case stronger.</p>

