        <!-- SECTION 4: The long-horizon argument (synthesis)                -->
        <!-- Pulls together retrospective + projective into mental model.    -->
        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <div class="section">
            <div class="section-intro">
                <div class="section-eyebrow">Takeaway</div>
                <h2>The long-horizon argument</h2>
                <p><em>Tying the historical evidence to the decision a reader is making today.</em></p>
            </div>

            <p class="prose"><strong>Volatility is a horizon-dependent concern.</strong> A 50% drawdown is terrifying at six-month horizons and noise at ten-year horizons. The historical record in &sect;2 above is the empirical version of this claim: every worst-timed lump-sum entry the page tests at horizons of seven years or longer produced more wealth than the S&amp;P 500 over the same period. The 2021 top has not reached that horizon, and at just under five years it trails &mdash; and the heatmap&rsquo;s five-year row shows most 2021 entries trailing so far, though not all (January 2021, for one, finished ahead).</p>

            <p class="prose">The horizon-dependence shows up sharply when the recent past is sliced at different windows. Through September 2026: at one year, the S&amp;P 500 is up about <strong>17%</strong> and bitcoin down about <strong>27%</strong>. At five years the two are almost level, bitcoin up about <strong>91%</strong> and the S&amp;P about <strong>90%</strong>. At ten, bitcoin&rsquo;s roughly <strong>13,600%</strong> dwarfs the S&amp;P&rsquo;s <strong>318%</strong>. Same asset, same comparator, three completely different stories &mdash; <em>the drama isn&rsquo;t in the asset, it&rsquo;s in the horizon you use to measure it.</em></p>

            <p class="prose"><strong>Time in market applies differently at different CAGRs.</strong> A 10%-CAGR asset doubles in about seven years. A 25%-CAGR asset doubles in under three. Compounding advantage over long horizons is exponential, not linear &mdash; which is why a relatively small allocation to a high-trend asset, held across years, can materially shift a portfolio outcome even when most of the portfolio is in conventional assets. <em>The relevant question isn&rsquo;t whether bitcoin is volatile (it is) or risky (it is) or might fail (it might).</em> It&rsquo;s whether a small position, held long enough, with a clear understanding of the trend, compounds to something that materially changes the holder&rsquo;s financial picture. The historical record says yes at every horizon the page tests.</p>

            <p class="prose"><strong>Bitcoin vs. gold is its own argument.</strong> Gold has rallied strongly over recent years on central-bank buying and currency-debasement concerns &mdash; the same long-cycle store-of-value rotation bitcoiners argue eventually benefits bitcoin disproportionately, given gold&rsquo;s ~$25T market cap versus bitcoin&rsquo;s roughly an order of magnitude smaller. The mechanics, the relative cycles, the ETP-cost asymmetry (GLDM vs. GLD), and the structural overlap with bitcoin&rsquo;s thesis all deserve a dedicated treatment rather than a sidebar here; see the <em>forthcoming Bitcoin vs. Gold page</em>. This page&rsquo;s comparison set is bitcoin vs. the equity indexes a typical retail or institutional investor would otherwise hold.</p>

            <p class="prose"><strong>Bitcoin&rsquo;s volatility itself is changing structurally.</strong> A pattern emerged in 2025 that didn&rsquo;t appear in earlier cycles: bitcoin posted a fresh all-time high (~$126K in October) while realized volatility printed decade-lows in the same window &mdash; new ATH and new ATL volatility, simultaneously. The MVRV ratio (market value to realized cost basis) has held steady around 2.0&times; through the recent top, against 4&ndash;6&times; peaks at the 2017 and 2021 highs. The institutional infrastructure has thickened: roughly <strong>6.4% of supply</strong> now sits in spot bitcoin ETPs; <strong>23 nation-states</strong> are net accumulators; <strong>49+ public companies</strong> hold corporate treasury positions of more than 1,000 BTC each. None of this guarantees the trend continues; all of it makes a sudden mean-reversion to earlier-cycle volatility behavior less likely. The asset that the ten-year backtests on this page are measuring is empirically migrating &mdash; slowly but visibly &mdash; from speculative-crypto behavior toward something closer to a high-vol equity allocation with a structural tailwind.</p>

            <p class="prose">As with the rest of this site, the right reading isn&rsquo;t <em>&ldquo;this is the answer.&rdquo;</em> The right reading is: <strong>here&rsquo;s the historical record and the trend the model expects from here &mdash; including the cases where the picture is less favorable.</strong> The decision about whether to take a position, how large, and over what horizon is yours. The page has done its job by giving you enough structural detail to make that decision with eyes open.</p>

            <!-- ─── Math & methodology appendix (collapsed by default) ─── -->
            <details class="bvsm-math-collapsible">
                <summary class="bvsm-math-summary">Math &amp; methodology &mdash; click to expand the data sources and formulas behind the calculators</summary>
                <div class="bvsm-math">

                    <p><strong>Bitcoin price data.</strong> Daily-close BTC/USD prices since the bitcoin genesis block (January 3, 2009), as embedded in the site-wide <code>PL_DATA</code> array. For the retrospective calculators on this page, the BTC price on each preset top date is interpolated from the daily data.</p>

                    <p><strong>S&amp;P 500 total return.</strong> The S&amp;P 500 Total Return index (<code>^SP500TR</code>: price return plus dividends reinvested), one close a month: the last trading day on or before the 28th, from January 2010. Between monthly samples the page interpolates.</p>

                    <p><strong>NASDAQ-100 total return.</strong> Sampled the same way, from the dividend-adjusted close of QQQ, the fund that tracks the NASDAQ-100, rebased to the index&rsquo;s level on 28 January 2010. The fund&rsquo;s 0.20% annual fee is left in, so the series runs slightly below the index itself. The NASDAQ-100 excludes financials and concentrates roughly 52&ndash;55% in information technology; long-run nominal CAGR is approximately <strong>16.3%</strong> over the 18-year window 2007&ndash;2026 (about <strong>20.8%</strong> over the ten years to September 2026, from the page&rsquo;s own series). The page uses NASDAQ-100 rather than the broader Composite because the institutional-investor literature treats the NASDAQ-100 as the standard tech-tilted comparator; the Composite&rsquo;s long-run CAGR sits a few percentage points lower.</p>

                    <p><strong>Gold.</strong> Not included as a comparator on this page in its current form. Gold operates on materially different drivers (central-bank demand, currency debasement, store-of-value rotation, ETF cost structure differences between GLDM and GLD, the 28% collectibles capital-gains tax rate on physical-gold ETPs in U.S. taxable accounts) and warrants a dedicated comparison rather than a sidebar treatment. See the <em>forthcoming Bitcoin vs. Gold page</em> for the dedicated treatment.</p>

                    <p><strong>Power Law trend.</strong> The site-wide reference Power Law trend used by every calculator on this site, <code>P(d) = 1.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;17</sup> &times; d<sup>5.77</sup></code>, where <code>d</code> is days since the bitcoin genesis block (January 3, 2009). The model is canonically associated with Matthew Me&zcaron;inskis (Porkopolis Economics, 2018) and Giovanni Santostasi (2018); R<sup>2</sup> exceeds 0.95 across the full 2010&ndash;2026 fit window. The same coefficients power the projections on the <a href="/the-power-law">Power Law page</a>, the <a href="/the-bitcoin-retirement">Bitcoin Retirement</a> page, and the <a href="/bitcoin-backed-mortgages">Bitcoin-Backed Mortgages</a> page. Critics including Adrian Morris (overfitting) and Tim Stolte/Amdax (log-time-scaling cointegration concerns) have published methodological critiques; defenders (Burger &amp; Santostasi; Fulgur Ventures) have published rebuttals showing the residuals are stationary and the out-of-sample fit has improved over six years of post-publication validation. Santostasi himself states the theory is falsifiable: sustained deviation from the trend for an extended period would invalidate it.</p>

                    <p><strong>Bitcoin&rsquo;s two forward lines.</strong> The projection chart prices bitcoin on the site&rsquo;s two scenarios: <code>P(t) = P<sub>trend</sub>(t) &times; m(t)</code>, with <code>m<sub>0</sub> = P<sub>today</sub> / P<sub>trend</sub>(today)</code>, today being the latest monthly sample. <strong>If bitcoin stays where it is against trend</strong>, <code>m(t) = m<sub>0</sub></code> throughout, so the amount invested grows by <code>P<sub>trend</sub>(t) / P<sub>trend</sub>(today)</code>. <strong>If it returns to trend over 3 years</strong>, <code>m(t)</code> moves in a straight line from <code>m<sub>0</sub></code> to 1.0 over 36 months and stays there. Both lines start at the amount invested. <em>Changed October 14, 2026:</em> the dashed line used to be the &ldquo;current-price&rdquo; projection, priced at the trend from year 0, so with bitcoin below trend it started at about 1.9&times; the amount invested before any time had passed. It now closes the gap over 3 years; the values at the 5-to-40-year horizons the slider offers are unchanged. The solid line, formerly the &ldquo;trend basis&rdquo;, is the same line under its new name.</p>

                    <p><strong>Comparator forward trends.</strong> For the projection chart, the comparator trends compound at their approximate historical nominal CAGRs: S&amp;P 500 at <strong>10.9%</strong> (1992&ndash;2026 total return per Damodaran/SP DJI) and NASDAQ-100 at <strong>16.3%</strong> (2007&ndash;2026 long-run; about 20.8% over the latest ten years). These are long-run historical averages, not forecasts. The Power Law trend&rsquo;s own growth rate stays above the NASDAQ-100&rsquo;s 16.3% until about 2047 and above the S&amp;P 500&rsquo;s 10.9% until about 2064; because bitcoin is so far ahead by then, the comparators take decades longer to catch up. <em>Approximate convergence dates</em> &mdash; the year by which the comparator&rsquo;s constant-CAGR trajectory catches bitcoin&rsquo;s, starting from the same amount today &mdash; are around <strong>2080 for the NASDAQ-100</strong> and <strong>2138 for the S&amp;P 500</strong> if bitcoin stays at today&rsquo;s multiple of trend, and about a decade later on each (2088 and 2148) if it returns to trend over 3 years. Computed October 2026 at 0.53&times; trend; the dates move with bitcoin&rsquo;s price. The practical implication isn&rsquo;t the year &mdash; it&rsquo;s that the bitcoin advantage persists for the entire 10-to-30-year horizon any current investor would meaningfully care about.</p>

                    <p><strong>The stress test: stocks since 2010.</strong> The second set of stock rates is each index&rsquo;s own total return from January 2010 to the latest monthly sample in the page&rsquo;s series (the S&amp;P 500 total-return index, and the NASDAQ-100 through QQQ&rsquo;s dividend-adjusted close): <strong><span data-bvsm="spRecent">14.6%</span></strong> and <strong><span data-bvsm="ndqRecent">19.5%</span></strong> a year through <span data-bvsm="recentEnd">September 2026</span>, computed as the page loads, so they move with each monthly sample. January 2010 is where the page&rsquo;s equity series and bitcoin&rsquo;s price record both begin, so the stress test compares the indices with bitcoin over the same years; a start at the March 2009 low would raise both rates further by picking the bottom. The stretch includes one of the strongest runs in US stock-market history, so the stress test makes the stock market&rsquo;s case stronger and bitcoin&rsquo;s lead smaller. At these rates the convergence dates come much sooner: about <strong>2062 for the NASDAQ-100</strong> and <strong>2092 for the S&amp;P 500</strong> if bitcoin stays at today&rsquo;s multiple of trend, and about a decade later on each (2070 and 2101) if it returns to trend over 3 years. Computed October 2026 at 0.53&times; trend. Within the chart&rsquo;s 40-year range, the NASDAQ-100&rsquo;s line passes the lower bitcoin line only in the last few years.</p>

                    <p><strong>What the calculators don&rsquo;t model.</strong> Tax treatment (pre-tax comparison only). Transaction costs and platform fees. Currency: USD throughout. Capital-gains realization on rebalancing. <strong>Survivorship bias</strong> &mdash; bitcoin has survived to be measured; many crypto peers (BCH, BSV, XRP, ETC and a long tail of altcoins) have not. Empirical research suggests omitting failed-asset returns inflates measured CAGRs by approximately <strong>0.9&ndash;2.1% per year</strong>; the bitcoin numbers on this page benefit from that bias by virtue of being bitcoin-specific, but readers comparing crypto-asset-class performance to equities should adjust the headline returns downward by that range. The lump-sum and DCA calculations assume the position was held without sale or rebalancing through to today.</p>

                </div>
